{
  "title": "Projector air filter lid (v0 estimate)",
  "summary": "A printable replacement for a missing air-filter lid on an ultra-short-throw projector. It holds two pieces cut from a 142 mm foam pad for fresh-air vents instead of the original filter: slot vents over the foam, and a clamp ring plus a seam rib that press the foam's edges onto the filter seat. A test ring checks the fit first. Shape study: dimensions are estimated from a photo and must be measured before printing.",
  "status": "preview",
  "date": "2026-11-26",
  "material": "PETG",
  "notes": "NOT READY TO PRINT. The outline, depths, hinge and catch positions are estimated from a listing photo scaled by the projector's published size (Epson's drawings confirm the scale to about 6 %, which is still too loose to print from). Filter: a Flexit 100FF pad (142 x 142 x 10 mm polyurethane foam, ePM coarse, sold in 4-packs). The stepped pocket is about 177 x 103 mm, wider than one pad, so it takes two pieces cut from one pad (92.5 x 78.5 and 86.9 x 62.4 mm, 1 mm oversize per side; media_template_flexit.svg in the job folder, print at 100 % and check the 100 mm bar). A 2.4 mm clamp ring (16.6 mm tall) presses the foam's edge down to 5 mm onto the seat all round, and a 2.4 mm seam rib holds the joint between the two pieces, so no air gets past the media. The model also has a one-piece fleece option (a 160 mm wide, 6 mm fleece roll or G3/G4 mat, squeezed to 3 mm, template media_template_fleece.svg): one setting in make_lid.py. Coarse foam filters about like G3 mat, less fine dust than Epson's pleated filter, with no airflow penalty. Don't use vacuum-bag fabric, paper, HEPA or coffee filters: they restrict a projector fan's airflow and make it run hot. The lid hinges along its straight edge and is held at the free edge by a sliding latch on the original. Here a flexing beam replaces the latch, and the two hinge hooks are placeholders until the body's hinge prongs have been seen close up. The vents open 7,296 mm2, 78 % of the media face, with 1.4 mm bars. The focus lever is inside this bay, so the lid has to be easy to open. PETG, 0.2 mm layers, 4 walls, 20 % gyroid, outer face on the textured plate, no supports. The intake side sees room air, so PLA would survive the airflow; PETG is for heat soak after shutdown and for the latch beam, which PLA would slowly creep under load. Test ring: 53 min, 20 g. Lid: 2 h 29 min, 73 g. Both are 226.8 mm wide, 13 mm from each bed edge.",
